<section class="hero">
  <h1>StructFS</h1>
  <p class="tagline">The driver system for modern applications</p>
  <p class="subtitle">Two operations. Structured data. Paths for everything.</p>
  <div class="hero-actions">
    <a href="/playground/" class="btn btn-primary">Try it</a>
    <a href="/concepts/" class="btn btn-secondary">The model</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="philosophy">
  <div class="philosophy-lead">
    <p>In the 1970s, every application had to know how to talk to every printer. Then operating systems introduced drivers &mdash; a uniform interface between applications and hardware &mdash; and the problem disappeared.</p>
    <p>Today we have the same problem with data. Every application has to know how to talk to every API, every database, every file format, every cloud service. The protocols differ. The conventions differ. The error handling differs. The testing story is always bespoke.</p>
    <p>StructFS is a driver layer for data. One interface. Two operations. Everything else is a store you plug in.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="features">
  <div class="feature-grid">
    <div class="feature-card">
      <h3>Read and write</h3>
      <p>Every interaction &mdash; files, HTTP, databases, OS primitives &mdash; is <code>read(path)</code> or <code>write(path, value)</code>. No other operations.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <h3>Structures, not bytes</h3>
      <p>Values are typed &mdash; strings, numbers, booleans, arrays, maps &mdash; not byte streams. No parsing, no encoding, no format negotiation.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <h3>Paths are the interface</h3>
      <p>A path names a resource, an action, or a capability. Composition happens by composing paths, not by inventing new verbs.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <h3>Stores all the way down</h3>
      <p>Anything that responds to read and write is a store. The tree is stores. Configuration is a store. Documentation is a store.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="philosophy" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
  <h2>What this makes possible</h2>
  <div class="philosophy-grid">
    <div>
      <h3>Test without mocking</h3>
      <p>Swap a store at a path. No network, no mock server, no test doubles for six client libraries.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Compose without glue</h3>
      <p>Mount stores at prefixes. Caching, routing, proxying &mdash; tree configuration, not application code.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Port without rewriting</h3>
      <p>Same logic, different stores. Local, remote, in-process. Change the wiring, keep the code.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

